!!Der!! sat on a frontier. It was a [[ Sumerian | sumer ]] [[ city-state ]] east of the [[ Tigris River ]], on the border between Sumer and [[ Elam ]], and its remains lie at modern !!Tell Aqar!! near [[ al-Badra | badra-iraq ]] in [[ Iraq ]]'s [[ Wasit Governorate ]]. In [[ Sumerian | sumerian-language ]] the name was written 𒌷𒂦𒀭𒆠 //^^uru^^BAD,,3,,.AN^^ki^^//;[[ ^1 ]] in [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] it was 𒌷𒂦𒀭𒆠 //^^uru^^BAD,,3,,.AN^^ki^^// or 𒌷𒁲𒂊𒊒(𒆠) //^^uru^^de-e-ru^^(ki)^^//. At one time it was thought that Der might have been ancient Durum ([[ Sumerian | sumerian-language ]]: //^^uru^^BAD,,3,,^^ki^^//), but more recent scholarship has rebutted that.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]]

The city's principal god was [[ Ištaran ]]. By the 1st millennium BC he was also being called //Anu rabû// ("//Great [[ Anu ]]//") in Akkadian, and his temple at Der was named Edimgalkalama.[[ ^5 ]]

== History



== Early Bronze

Occupation at Der runs from the [[ Early Dynastic period | early-dynastic-period-mesopotamia ]] through [[ Neo-Assyrian ]] times. The city's local deity was [[ Ishtaran ]], and he was represented on Earth by his minister, the snake god Nirah.

== Ur III period

In the late 3rd millennium Der was mentioned twice during the reign of [[ Sulgi ]] of the [[ Third Dynasty of Ur ]], and the two mentions are ten years apart and could hardly be more different. Sulgi's year name 11 was "Year Ishtaran of Der was brought into his temple"; year 21 was "Year Der was destroyed". Under [[ Amar-Sin ]], while the king was away on a long military campaign against Huhnuri, the crown prince [[ Shu-Sin ]] left his post in Der to return and hold Ur.[[ ^7 ]]

== Middle Bronze

In the second millennium Der turns up in a tablet discovered at [[ Mari | mari-syria ]], sent by [[ Yarim-Lim I ]] of [[ Yamhad ]]. It reminds Yasub-Yahad, king of Der, of the military help Yarim-Lim had given him for fifteen years, and then declares war on the city, in retaliation for what Yarim-Lim described as evil deeds committed by Yasub-Yahad.[[ ^8 ]] Other kings recorded doing Der damage. [[ Rim-Sin I ]] of [[ Larsa ]] reported destroying it in his 20th year. [[ Ammi-Ditana ]] of [[ Babylon ]] recorded destroying the city wall of Der in his 37th year, a wall he said had been built earlier by [[ Damqi-ilishu ]] of the [[ Sealand Dynasty ]].

One of Der's own rulers speaks in an inscription. Ilum-muttabbil, a little known ruler of the early Old Babylonian period, claimed to have defeated the armies of [[ Anshan | anshan-persia ]], Elam, and Simaski, in alliance with [[ Marhaši | marhasi ]].[[ ^9 ]]

== Iron Age

In 720 BC the [[ Assyrian | assyria ]] king [[ Sargon II ]] moved against Elam, and it was near Der that the Assyrian host was defeated, by the combined army of king Humban-Nikash I of Elam and king [[ Marduk-apla-iddina II ]] of [[ Babylon ]].[[ ^10 ]] Nearly two centuries later, following the Persian conquest of Babylon in 539 BC, the [[ Cyrus Cylinder ]] names Der among the cities whose people were repatriated and whose god's sanctuary was restored.

== Archaeology

It appears that no excavation has ever taken place at Der. Several notable objects have nonetheless been discovered nearby, including a [[ kudurru ]] (found in Sippar) which confirmed the name of the site.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^12 ]] The site itself has been heavily damaged by water over the centuries, and was considered not worth excavating.[[ ^13 ]]

